WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:10.100
Usman dan Fodio, a Fulani scholar and preacher, lived and taught at Degel, in the Hausa kingdom of Gobir.

2
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:16.600
Gobir's king, Yunfa, turned against him. In February 1804 Usman and his community left Degel for Gudu, about 48 km to the north-west: their hijra.

3
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:23.100
At Gudu his followers named him Amir al-Mu'minin, commander of the faithful. On 21 February 1804 Yunfa declared war on him.

4
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:29.600
On 21 June 1804, at the lake of Kwatto, the jihad's army defeated Yunfa's. Where the lake lay is not certain, so no place is drawn.

5
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:35.600
Across Hausaland, leaders who carried Usman's flag rose against their kings. Zaria fell to the jihad in 1805.

6
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:41.600
In 1807 jihad forces captured Kano, and an emirate replaced its Hausa king.

7
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:47.600
On 3 October 1808 Alkalawa, Gobir's capital, fell, and Yunfa was killed.

8
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.000
In 1809 Usman's son Muhammad Bello turned the camp at Sokoto into a town. It became the seat of the caliphate.

9
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.000
By 1812 Usman's brother Abdullahi ruled from Gwandu, west of Sokoto.

10
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:03.000
Usman dan Fodio died at Sokoto on 20 April 1817. His son Muhammad Bello became caliph.

11
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:11.500
Abdullahi at first refused to recognise Bello. The caliphate came to be run in two halves: the eastern emirates answered to Sokoto, the western and southern ones to Gwandu, under the caliph.

12
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:21.000
Each emir was appointed by the caliph and ruled with wide self-rule. Every year he travelled to pledge allegiance and bring tribute: crops, cowries and enslaved people.

13
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:29.000
In the south, Afonja, who had taken Ilorin out of the Oyo empire, was killed by his allies. Abd al-Salam became Ilorin's first emir, under Gwandu.

14
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:37.000
In 1841, after several moves, Modibbo Adama settled the capital of Adamawa at Yola, far to the east. He had led its jihad since 1809.

15
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:43.400
From 1897 the caliphate was attacked from the south.

16
00:01:43.400 --> 00:02:02.300
In January 1897 a Royal Niger Company force under George Goldie took Bida, the Nupe capital, and deposed its emir; Ilorin's emir then submitted to the company.

17
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:08.000
On 1 January 1900 the company handed its territories to the British government, and on paper the emirates lay inside a British protectorate.

18
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:17.000
British forces took Zaria in 1901.

19
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:23.100
A force under Colonel Thomas Morland left Zaria at the end of January 1903 and took Kano on 3 February after heavy fighting.

20
00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.500
Sokoto fell after a battle on 15 March.

21
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.000
On 21 March Lugard named a new sultan, Attahiru II, and emirs who accepted British rule were kept in office under British officers.

22
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.000
Caliph Attahiru I had left Sokoto with his followers. British forces caught up with them at Burmi, in today's Gombe State, where he and many of his followers were killed on 27 July 1903.
